    "description": "Iranian transport sources distinguish freeways from highways and other interurban roads. The Tehran–North project illustrates the engineering complexity of mountain freeway construction and the importance of distinguishing individual section openings from completion of an entire route. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Freeways", "Road infrastructure", "Mountain engineering", "Tehran–North", "Transport"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:40:18Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:40:18Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iranian Freeways: Road Classes and Mountain Construction | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s freeway category through the Tehran–North project, its mountain engineering and the distinction between section openings and route completion."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "distinct-road-class",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A distinct part of the road system",
        "content": "Iran’s freeway network is a specific component of its wider road system. An Iranian chamber-of-commerce presentation hosted by the United Nations eTIR programme lists freeways separately from highways, major roads, minor roads and rural roads. Consequently, a national road-length figure should not be described as freeway mileage, and figures using different road classifications should not be combined without checking their definitions. The presentation establishes the distinction but does not provide a sufficiently documented annual freeway series for this article. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "tehran-north-case",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Tehran–North: opening a section is not completing a route",
        "content": "Tehran–North provides a concrete example of freeway development through mountainous terrain. Iran Press reported the opening of its first phase on 25 February 2020. That report described tunnels, ventilation equipment, access tunnels and bridges, while identifying other phases as unfinished. Its claims about exceptional safety were attributed to project personnel; they are not treated here as independently demonstrated results. [s2]\n\nThe Iranian Tunnelling Association’s activity report for 2024 subsequently continued to list Tehran–North among road projects under construction. It distinguished sections completed in both directions from sections completed in one direction. This distinction matters: an inauguration can establish that a particular carriageway or section has opened without establishing uninterrupted operation of the entire planned freeway. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "engineering-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the engineering evidence",
        "content": "The tunnelling report places Tehran–North within a wider programme of underground construction. For this freeway, tunnels are central to the alignment rather than incidental structures. The source supports an account of substantial mountain engineering, but it does not establish current travel times, traffic volumes, maintenance performance or crash outcomes. [s3]\n\nThe inspected reports also give different overall project lengths. Without an engineering document reconciling their measurement boundaries, neither figure is adopted as a definitive route total. A fuller national account requires a dated inventory of operating sections, their road classifications and their opening status. Until that evidence is assembled, Tehran–North is best understood here as a documented construction case rather than a proxy for the performance of all Iranian freeways. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
